                                                Hash Table




A hash function needs to be consistent(same output for same input every time)
no output will be same for 2 different inputs
Hash function also knows how big your array is, so it only returns valid indexes only

//Simplification
The hash function outputs “3”. So let’s store the price of an apple at
index 3 in the array.
Let’s add milk. Feed “milk”
into the hash function.
The hash function says “0”. Let’s store the price of milk at index 0.


Mapping web addresses to IPs,called DNS resolution, hash table are one way to implement this functionality
Hash tables are also used as caches

->Collisions: When 2 keys have been assigned same slot
In reality, its almost impossible to make a hash function that is 100% consistent

SOlution: Start a linked list at that collided node
If that linked list gets very long, theres no use of it until and unless we have a good hash function

O(1)->Doesnt mean an instant, it means that the time taken will be same for any input size

TO avoid collisions, we need a low load factor, and a good hash function
Load factor is (Number of items in a hash table)/(total number of slots)

Lower teh load factor, the lesser the collisions
A good hash function distributes values in an array evenly
Hash tables are good for catching duplicates
